  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "0N+TGZMT9NZ737kdhmMDN+xr4DA=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:43:36.438Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "When a package is published the registry can tell subscribers in real time, two ways.\n\n**SSE \u2014 `GET /-/events`.** A persistent connection receiving a `connected` event on open, then a `publish` event per publish with `{ \"packageName\": \"@scope/pkg\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\" }`. In a browser that is a plain `EventSource` with an event listener.\n\n**Webhooks**, which are **persisted to disk and survive restarts**, and can additionally be supplied through configuration:\n\n- `GET /-/webhooks` \u2014 all registered webhooks, predefined plus dynamic;\n- `POST /-/webhooks` \u2014 register `{ endpoint, headers? }`, returning `201`; **duplicate endpoints are ignored** rather than erroring;\n- `DELETE /-/webhooks` \u2014 remove a dynamic webhook by endpoint, `204` on success and `404` if unknown. **Predefined webhooks cannot be removed** \u2014 a deliberate asymmetry, since a configured integration should not be deletable by whoever can reach the API.\n\nEach webhook receives a POST when a package is published.\n\nThe pairing matters for a host: SSE is right for a UI that wants to react while a user is looking at it, webhooks for automation that must not miss an event while nothing is connected.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/registry/README.md` \u2014 Publish Notifications \u203a SSE and Webhooks\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:43:36.438Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:43:36.438Z"
    }
  },
